- existing single-item update/validation: `setPriority(id: string, priority: number)` at source/index.ts:837-852:
```
setPriority(id: string, priority: number) {
    if (typeof priority !== 'number' || !Number.isFinite(priority)) {
        throw new TypeError(`Expected \`priority\` to be a finite number, got \`${priority}\` (${typeof priority})`);
    }

    // Checked here, not only in `PriorityQueue`, so that it also throws with a custom queue class.
    for (const waitingTask of this.#waitingTasks) {
        if (waitingTask.id === id) {
            this.#queue.setPriority(id, priority);
            waitingTask.priority = priority;
            return;
        }
    }

    throw new ReferenceError(`No promise function with the id "${id}" exists in the queue.`);
}
```
  JSDoc above it (source/index.ts:802-804) states it changes only a waiting task, and throws `ReferenceError` if no waiting task has the id (e.g. already started) and `TypeError` for non-finite priority.

2. Queue interface — actual location: source/queue.ts, whole file (lines 1-10):
```
export type RunFunction = () => Promise<unknown>;

export type Queue<Element, Options> = {
    size: number;
    filter: (options: Readonly<Partial<Options>>) => Element[];
    dequeue: () => Element | undefined;
    enqueue: (run: Element, options?: Partial<Options>) => void;
    setPriority: (id: string, priority: number) => void;
    remove: (id: string) => void;
};
```
A per-queue bulk method would be declared in this `Queue` type in source/queue.ts. Note it is a type alias, not an `interface`. The generated declarations come from tsc into `dist/` (no hand-written `.d.ts`). Implementers of `Queue` in the repo: `PriorityQueue` (source/priority-queue.ts:11), `FifoQueue` in test/helpers.ts:19, `FifoQueue` in test/fixtures/types.ts:63, `GroupQueue` in test/fixtures/types.ts:172, and the readme example `FifoQueue` at readme.md:821-855.

3. PriorityQueue class (source/priority-queue.ts)
- `export type PriorityQueueOptions = {priority?: number | undefined} & QueueAddOptions;` lines 7-9.
- `export default class PriorityQueue implements Queue<RunFunction, PriorityQueueOptions>` line 11.
- storage: `readonly #queue: Array<PriorityQueueOptions & {run: RunFunction} | undefined> = []` and `#head = 0` lines 13-16; live items are indices `[#head, length)`.
- `enqueue(run, options?)` lines 58-90:
  - `const {priority = 0, id} = options ?? {};` :59-61.
  - if logically empty, reset array and push; :71-76.
  - if `this.#queue.at(-1)!.priority! >= priority`, append to tail; comment :79-82: "Appending preserves FIFO order for equal priorities."
  - otherwise compact and do a `lowerBound` binary insertion descending by priority, with `a!.priority === b!.priority ? 0 : b!.priority! - a!.priority!` (equal priorities as `0` to avoid `Infinity - Infinity === NaN`): :86-89.
  - therefore dequeue order is highest priority first; equal priorities are FIFO (insertion order).
- `setPriority(id, priority)` lines 92-105:
```
setPriority(id: string, priority: number) {
    const index = this.#findIndex(element => element.id === id);
    if (index === -1) {
        throw new ReferenceError(`No promise function with the id "${id}" exists in the queue.`);
    }

    // Moving it would put it behind the tasks with the same priority.
    if (this.#queue[index]!.priority === priority) {
        return;
    }

    const [item] = this.#queue.splice(index, 1);
    this.enqueue(item!.run, {priority, id});
}
```
  It works only on live items (safe for unknown id after dequeue tested in test/priority-queue.ts:16-35).
- `dequeue(): RunFunction | undefined` lines 120-122; it delegates to private `#removeFirst()` (40-56) and returns `undefined` when empty.
- `remove(idOrRun)` overloads lines 107-118: if the match is at `#head` remove is O(1); otherwise splice.
- `filter(options)` lines 124-135: matches only `element.priority === options.priority` and returns the run functions.
- `size` getter lines 137-139: `this.#queue.length - this.#head`.

4. Waiting vs running tasks and id validation (yes, designed for p-queue)
- WaitingTask type: source/index.ts:81-90; set of waiting tasks: :154. RunningTask shape: :143-148.
- A task is "waiting" after enqueue until its run wrapper starts; the run wrapper at :641-644 does `waitingTask.stopListeningForAbort?.()` and `this.#waitingTasks.delete(waitingTask)`, then :653-662 adds a separate runningTask object to `#runningTasks`. On completion the finally block deletes that runningTask: :725-726.
- IDs are not validated for duplicates at `add` time; `#idAssigner` guarantees unique defaults only (:140, :615). The only id lookup/validation is in `PQueue.setPriority` (:837-852): it searches `#waitingTasks` by `waitingTask.id`, calls `#queue.setPriority`, updates `waitingTask.priority`, and otherwise throws `ReferenceError('No promise function with the id "X" exists in the queue.')`.
- Running-task id: `PQueue.setPriority` never looks at `#runningTasks`; because a running task was removed from `#waitingTasks` at :644, calling `setPriority` on a running id falls through to the same `ReferenceError` as an unknown id. `PriorityQueue.setPriority` has its own `ReferenceError` for ids not in the queued (waiting) data structure (:93-96), but `PQueue` pre-checks `#waitingTasks` so custom queue classes also get the correct behavior.

